 # Opacity-based Early Termination
+
 ## Overview
-Opacity-based Early Termination is a family of volume rendering optimizations that halt the front-to-back ray integration loop once the accumulated opacity exceeds a predefined threshold, thereby skipping samples that contribute negligibly to the final pixel color [S1]. It differs from [Empty-space skipping](1002-empty-space-skipping.md) by operating on the accumulated transmittance of the ray path rather than spatially culling empty voxels, and from [Russian Roulette](1371-russian-roulette.md) by using a deterministic cutoff rather than a probabilistic one [G].
+Opacity-based Early Termination is a family of volume rendering optimizations that halt the front-to-back ray integration loop once the accumulated opacity exceeds a predefined threshold, thereby skipping samples that contribute negligibly to the final pixel color [S1]. It differs from [Empty-space skipping](1002-empty-space-skipping.md) by operating on the accumulated transmittance of the ray path rather than spatially culling empty voxels, and from [Russian Roulette](1371-russian-roulette.md) by using a deterministic cutoff rather than a probabilistic one, which introduces a systematic bias in exchange for reduced noise and computational cost [G].
+
 ## Shared principles
-Builds on [Early Termination and Probabilistic Path Control](1791-early-termination-and-probabilistic-path-control.md) [G].
+The core mechanism shared by all methods in this family is the reduction of work units (samples, voxels, or pixels) processed per frame while maintaining acceptable visual fidelity [G]. This is achieved through three primary strategies:
+1. **Culling/Skipping:** Identifying regions of the volume or ray path that do not contribute to the final image (e.g., empty space, occluded voxels) and bypassing their evaluation [S2].
+2. **Termination:** Halting the integration loop when the accumulated contribution becomes negligible (e.g., high opacity, low transmittance) [S1].
+3. **Adaptation:** Varying the computational effort based on local scene complexity, as measured by color difference in sample regions [S1].
+
+Mathematically, these methods modify the standard front-to-back compositing loop:
+$$ C_{acc} = C_{acc} + C_{sample} \cdot \alpha_{sample} \cdot (1 - \alpha_{acc}) $$
+$$ \alpha_{acc} = \alpha_{acc} + \alpha_{sample} \cdot (1 - \alpha_{acc}) $$
+by introducing conditions that break the loop early, skip iterations, or reduce the number of $C_{sample}$ queries [G].
+
